.TH CDYNMQ_ENABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cdynmq_enable_sending \- turn sends on again on a dynamic queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval cdynmq_enable_sending(cdynmq *dq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Clears the flag that cdynmq_disable_sending() set, so sends are
accepted again instead of returning ccol_not_permitted. A new queue
starts with sends on, so you only need this call after sends were
turned off.

It wakes every write-direction waiter on the queue (a cselect()
call, a cselect_timed() call or a ceventloop registration),
and each of them scans again and sees the new state. While sends are
off the queue is ready for the write direction (see
cdynmq_disable_sending(3)); with sends on, it is ready while it
holds fewer than ccol_max_elem_count messages.

Turning sends off and on again never drops anything: The messages that
were in the queue before stay as they were.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue that gets sends turned on
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - Sends are on again
ccol_invalid_args  - dq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cdynmq_disable_sending (3),
.BR cdynmq_send (3),
.BR cdynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR ccircq_enable_sending (3)
